// Chapter framing for each topic's Learn page: an opening (what the topic is and why it matters) and a closing
// takeaway. Written from the topic's own key ideas, analogy, common mistake and interview line; they add no new claims.
// Checked by tools/check-content.mjs.
window.SD = window.SD || {};
SD.frame = {
 "0A.1": {
  "intro": "Functional requirements are the list of things the system lets someone do. Each one is a capability seen from the outside: an action a user, an admin or another service can take, with nothing said about speed or how it is built.\n\nEverything you draw later hangs off this list. Each requirement turns into an endpoint and a path through your diagram, so a bloated list gives you a shallow design, and breadth with no depth reads as poor judgment. The key ideas below cover how to phrase requirements, how many to commit to, who the actors are, and how the list works as a checklist.",
  "takeaway": "The thread through all of this is commitment. A small, agreed set of flows phrased as things users can do gives you a scope you can finish and a way to check your own design, because each flow must appear as an endpoint and a traceable path. Naming the actors and parking the rest keeps that scope honest. If you remember one thing: pick about three core flows, say what is out of scope, and get agreement before you design."
 },
 "0A.2": {
  "intro": "Non-functional requirements describe how well the system must do its job. They are the qualities you can measure: how quickly it answers, how much load it takes, how often it is up, and whether acknowledged data can ever be lost.\n\nThese are what shape an architecture, because every component you add later should exist to meet one of them. Reciting the whole list without priorities gives no basis for trade-offs, and different products want opposite ones. The key ideas below cover the common qualities, putting numbers on them, what the nines mean, durability versus availability, and choosing the few that matter.",
  "takeaway": "What ties these ideas together is that a quality only becomes a requirement once it has a number and a priority. A measurable target tells you whether a design passes, and a short ranked list tells you what you are allowed to give up to reach it. That is what lets you justify each component later. If you remember one thing: choose about three qualities that drive this design, quantify them, and state what you will trade for them."
 },
 "0A.3": {
  "intro": "Capacity estimation is rough arithmetic about load: how many requests per second, how much storage, how much bandwidth. You start from a few assumptions about users and usage and round hard until you have an order of magnitude.\n\nThe point is to learn which dimension is hard, because that decides where the design effort goes. Done badly, it becomes minutes of silent division that changes nothing, and some interviewers treat the step as optional. The key ideas below cover the goal, anchors for converting daily volume to QPS, peak multipliers, the storage and bandwidth formulas, and ending on a consequence.",
  "takeaway": "Every idea here serves one purpose: turning a rough number into a design decision. The anchors and formulas make the arithmetic quick, the peak multiplier keeps you from sizing for an average that does not describe the busy period, and the closing consequence is what makes the exercise worth the time. If you remember one thing: round hard, say your assumptions aloud, and finish every estimate by stating what it means for the design."
 },
 "0A.4": {
  "intro": "Constraints and assumptions are the boundaries of the problem. Some are handed to you by the interviewer, some you decide yourself because nobody specified them, and some things you set aside entirely. Making that sorting visible is the whole topic.\n\nIt matters because a design is only right relative to its boundaries. If you quietly assume a scale the prompt never asked for, you can build something far larger than needed and never learn why it landed badly. The key ideas below cover the three piles, stating assumptions aloud, the typical constraints to check, and how to budget your time across the round.",
  "takeaway": "The common thread is making the frame of the problem explicit so it can be corrected. An assumption that is spoken and written down can be challenged in seconds, while a hidden one surfaces late as a design that answers the wrong question. Time is one more constraint, and it deserves the same deliberate budget. If you remember one thing: say every assumption out loud and invite the interviewer to correct it."
 },
 "0A.5": {
  "intro": "Clarifying questions are the short round of questions you ask before designing anything. Their job is to pin down the facts that would send the design in a different direction: scale, the read:write ratio, and who the users are.\n\nThis step shows whether you can separate what you need to be told from what you are expected to decide. Many questions with no proposals hand the interview to the interviewer and use up your time. The key ideas below cover which questions are worth asking, which ones to avoid, pairing each with a default, and keeping the whole thing short.",
  "takeaway": "What links these ideas is that a question is only worth asking if its answer changes what you build, and that you stay in charge while asking. Constraints come from the interviewer; component choices are yours to make and defend. Attaching a default to each question keeps you moving whatever the answer. If you remember one thing: ask a handful of design-changing questions in about five minutes, each with an assumption you will proceed on."
 },
 "0B.1": {
  "intro": "REST is a style of HTTP API in which you expose things as resources with addresses and act on them through a small fixed set of HTTP verbs. The response status code tells the caller what happened.\n\nThe conventions matter because clients, proxies and monitoring all rely on them. An error hidden inside a successful response is invisible to everything that reads the status code, and an API without versions cannot change without breaking its callers. The key ideas below cover resource naming, what each verb means and which are idempotent, the status codes to know, pagination, and versioning.",
  "takeaway": "The thread is that REST works by convention: paths name things, verbs say what is done to them, and status codes report the outcome, so every tool between client and server can understand a request without reading its body. Idempotency rules, pagination and versioning apply the same discipline to retries, long lists and change over time. If you remember one thing: let the verb and the status code carry the meaning, not the path or the response body."
 },
 "0B.2": {
  "intro": "An RPC framework lets one service call a typed function on another. gRPC is one such framework: calls travel over HTTP/2, messages are compact binary Protocol Buffers by default, and both sides generate their code from a shared schema.\n\nIt is easy to misapply. Choosing it for a public API because it is faster ignores the limits browsers place on it and how hard binary payloads are to debug. The key ideas below cover what gRPC is made of, its four call types, where it fits well and poorly, and how to evolve a schema safely.",
  "takeaway": "What connects these ideas is the shared contract. A schema both sides generate code from gives you strict types, low overhead, streaming and deadlines that can travel down the call chain, and that same requirement makes gRPC awkward for browsers and third parties. The schema evolution rules exist to keep the contract intact as it changes. If you remember one thing: use gRPC between internal services, and expose something with more reach and easier debugging to the public."
 },
 "0B.3": {
  "intro": "GraphQL is an API style with a single endpoint. Instead of calling a fixed URL per resource, the client sends a query that describes the exact shape of the data it wants, and the server returns that shape.\n\nIt solves a specific problem: clients receiving too many fields, or needing too many round trips, which hurts mobile clients and products with many different front-ends. It also brings costs, and adopting it for a simple CRUD API with one client buys complexity without benefit. The key ideas below cover the query model, what it fixes, the N+1 problem, its costs, and federation.",
  "takeaway": "The thread is that GraphQL moves control over the response shape from the server to the client, and the other ideas follow from that shift. Clients get exactly what they need, while the server takes on new work: batching child lookups, limiting expensive queries, and coping with caching that a single URL makes harder. If you remember one thing: reach for GraphQL when several clients need different slices of the same data, and plan batching and complexity limits from the start."
 },
 "0B.4": {
  "intro": "WebSocket and Server-Sent Events are two ways for a server to send data to a client without the client asking each time. A WebSocket is one long-lived connection that carries messages both ways. SSE is a one-way stream from server to client over plain HTTP.\n\nYou need them for chat, collaboration, feeds, notifications and streamed LLM output. The hard part is what long-lived connections imply: servers hold state, clients disconnect and come back, and a restart drops everyone at once. The key ideas below cover each protocol, stateful connections, reconnection with replay, and backpressure for slow clients.",
  "takeaway": "What ties this together is that a long-lived connection is state, and state has to be located, restored and bounded. You need to know which server holds which user, drain connections on deploy, resume from the last message a client saw, and stop a slow client from growing a buffer without limit. If you remember one thing: picking the protocol is the easy part, so design for the moment every client reconnects at the same instant."
 },
 "0B.5": {
  "intro": "An API gateway is a single entry point that sits in front of your backend services. Every request passes through it, and it handles the work common to all of them, such as TLS termination, authentication, rate limiting and routing.\n\nIt is easy to draw carelessly. Because it is on every request path, a single gateway box is a single point of failure for the whole system, and one that absorbs domain logic becomes a monolith. The key ideas below cover what a gateway does, what it buys you, what to keep out of it, how to run it safely, and the per-client variant.",
  "takeaway": "The thread is a clear boundary: the gateway owns concerns shared by every request and nothing that belongs to your domain. Holding that line keeps backend services simple, lets internal topology change without breaking clients, and stops everyone having to deploy through one component. Being on every path also means it must be redundant, with shared state held externally. If you remember one thing: keep the gateway thin and never run just one."
 },
 "0B.6": {
  "intro": "An operation is idempotent when performing it several times has the same effect as performing it once. It is a property you design into an API so that a repeated request does no extra harm.\n\nYou need it because networks time out and clients retry, and a timeout does not tell you whether the first attempt succeeded. Without idempotency, a retry can apply the same effect twice, such as charging a payment again. The key ideas below cover the definition, idempotency keys, storing the key atomically with the side effect, how exactly-once effects are usually achieved, and naturally idempotent updates.",
  "takeaway": "All of these ideas answer one situation: a request whose outcome the caller never learned. The key lets the server recognise a repeat, storing it in the same transaction as the effect closes the race between checking and acting, and updates phrased as setting a value are safe to repeat by nature. Together they make at-least-once delivery produce exactly-once effects. If you remember one thing: assume every write will be retried, and make the retry return the original result."
 },
 "0C.1": {
  "intro": "The box diagram is the high-level picture of your system: the clients, services, data stores and the connections between them. It shows enough to follow any request from start to finish and leaves out the rest.\n\nHow you build this drawing shows your judgment. Opening with many boxes invites a question about each one you cannot justify, while starting minimal and growing it lets every addition be explained. The key ideas below cover the simplest starting design, tracing requirements through it, when to add components, keeping services stateless, and labelling arrows.",
  "takeaway": "The thread running through these ideas is justification. You begin with the smallest design that meets the functional requirements, check it by tracing each requirement as a request path, and add a component only when a requirement or a number calls for it. Labelled arrows and stateless services make the remaining decisions visible. If you remember one thing: every box on the diagram should be there because you can say what demanded it."
 },
 "0C.2": {
  "intro": "A data model is your decision about what the system stores and how it is organised: the entities, how they relate, and which keys and indexes let you find them. It includes the choice of store.\n\nThe usual failure is picking a store for a slogan about scale without naming the access pattern that makes it fit, or without noticing the query that has just become impossible. The key ideas below cover starting from queries, naming the key for each one, when relational is the default, when key-value or wide-column fits, and deliberate denormalisation.",
  "takeaway": "What ties these ideas together is that storage follows the questions you will ask of it. Listing the queries tells you the keys, the keys tell you the partitioning and indexes, and the pattern of access tells you whether a relational store or a key-based one fits. Denormalising is the same reasoning applied to read-heavy paths. If you remember one thing: name the main query and the key it uses before you name a database."
 },
 "0C.3": {
  "intro": "The read path is the route a request takes when it fetches data, and the write path is the route it takes when it changes data. This topic is about designing them as two separate flows.\n\nIt matters because the two usually differ enormously in volume and in how fast they must be, so one design rarely suits both. A single arrow between service and database hides that asymmetry. The key ideas below cover drawing the paths separately, the tools for each, how the read:write ratio guides spending, and moving work from read time to write time.",
  "takeaway": "The thread is asymmetry. Once you see that reads and writes carry different loads, you can give each its own tools and decide which side should bear the cost. When reads dominate, extra effort on each write, such as precomputing what readers will ask for, makes the common case trivial. If you remember one thing: state the read:write ratio and let it tell you which path to make cheap."
 },
 "0C.4": {
  "intro": "A synchronous step is one the caller waits for before getting a reply. An asynchronous step is accepted, acknowledged, and finished later through a queue. The topic is choosing which work goes on each side.\n\nGet it wrong one way and users wait on slow work; get it wrong the other way and you defer a result the user needs on the next screen, then add polling to get it back. The key ideas below cover when to use each, what async gains and costs, what it requires, and a rule of thumb.",
  "takeaway": "What links these ideas is a single test: whether the user needs the result in order to continue. Work that passes the test stays in the request; everything else can be queued, which gives faster replies, burst absorption and failure isolation in exchange for eventual consistency, harder debugging, idempotent consumers and a dead-letter queue. If you remember one thing: do the minimum synchronously to return a correct response and queue the rest."
 },
 "0D.1": {
  "intro": "A bottleneck is the one point in a system that limits how much load the whole thing can carry. Bottleneck identification means finding that point by following a request through each hop and comparing what arrives with what the hop can handle.\n\nThe skill is finding it with reasoning instead of instinct. A common failure is scaling the part you know best when a different part is saturated, which changes nothing. The key ideas below cover walking the hops, why only the tightest one matters, where it usually sits, arguing with numbers, and naming the next one.",
  "takeaway": "The thread is that capacity is set by the hop with the least headroom, so effort anywhere else is wasted. Comparing load with capacity at each hop finds it, a stated capacity assumption turns the claim into an argument, and the stateful tier is the likely suspect because it cannot grow by adding more servers. Fixing it reveals the next. If you remember one thing: show the bottleneck with numbers before you propose scaling anything."
 },
 "0D.2": {
  "intro": "A failure mode is a specific way a component can go wrong, together with what happens to the rest of the system when it does. Analysing failure modes means going through your design box by box and working out what breaks, how far the damage reaches, and how service comes back.\n\nPeople who consider only crashes miss the failures that actually cause cascades: slowness, partial failure and overload. The key ideas below cover the three questions to ask of every box, why slow is often worse than dead, blast radius, removing single points of failure, graceful degradation, and recovery targets.",
  "takeaway": "What ties these ideas together is containment. You assume each component will fail in some way, including slowly or wrongly, and design so the effect stays small: timeouts and isolation limit the spread, redundancy and failover remove single points, and a partial answer replaces an error. Recovery targets say how good that has to be. If you remember one thing: for every box, ask what happens when it is slow, not only when it is dead."
 },
 "0D.3": {
  "intro": "Scaling a bottleneck means adding capacity at the point that limits the system. There are several ways to do it: a bigger machine, more machines, a cache, read replicas, sharding, or a queue. Each one addresses a different kind of limit.\n\nThe skill is matching the technique to the constraint. A technique applied to the wrong problem adds complexity and fixes nothing, and reaching first for sharding leaves you with permanent complexity where a cheaper tool would have bought a lot of time. The key ideas below take each of the six techniques in turn and say when it helps and when it does not.",
  "takeaway": "The thread is diagnosis before treatment. Each technique answers one kind of constraint, such as repeated reads, read volume, write volume or data size, or burstiness, and does little for the others. Naming the constraint first is what lets you pick the cheapest tool that addresses it and explain why the heavier ones can wait. If you remember one thing: state what the constraint is, then choose the simplest technique that targets exactly that."
 },
 "0D.4": {
  "intro": "Consistency here means a reader never gets a wrong or stale answer. Availability means the system gives an answer at all. When parts of a system cannot reach each other, you cannot keep both, and you have to decide which one to give up.\n\nThe weak answer to this trade-off is a single label for the whole system. A product that takes payments but is declared to favour availability everywhere has a problem. The key ideas below cover when the choice is actually forced, when to choose each side, why the decision is made per operation, and how conflicts are handled under eventual consistency.",
  "takeaway": "What connects these ideas is the cost of being wrong compared with the cost of not answering. The question only bites when a timeout fires and you must answer or refuse, and the right response depends on the operation: refuse where a wrong answer is expensive, answer where a stale one is harmless. Choosing availability then obliges you to say how conflicts resolve. If you remember one thing: decide consistency versus availability per operation, not once for the whole system."
 },
 "0D.5": {
  "intro": "Monitoring and observability are how you know whether a running system is healthy and, when it is not, how you find out why. In practice this means choosing what to measure, setting targets for it, deciding what should page a person, and collecting the telemetry needed for debugging.\n\nThe usual mistake is alerting on a cause such as CPU, which wakes people for non-problems and misses real outages where CPU is fine. The key ideas below cover the golden signals, the RED and USE methods, SLIs, SLOs and error budgets, symptom-based alerting, and percentiles and the kinds of telemetry.",
  "takeaway": "The thread is the user's experience as the reference point. Signals and methods tell you what to measure, the SLO turns a measurement into a target with a budget for failure, and alerts fire only on symptoms users feel, leaving causes on dashboards for diagnosis. Percentiles serve the same aim better than averages do. If you remember one thing: alert on symptoms measured against an SLO, and keep causes on the dashboard."
 },
 "0D.6": {
  "intro": "Cost optimisation is the work of understanding what a system costs to run and reducing it. The bill comes from three places: compute, storage and data transfer. Which of them dominates differs from one system to the next.\n\nThe usual mistake is trimming a small cost line while the dominant one goes untouched, or forgetting data transfer out to the internet entirely. The key ideas below cover finding the dominant line, committing to a steady baseline and autoscaling for peaks, spot instances and their risk, tiering storage by access frequency, and the levers for compute and data transfer.",
  "takeaway": "What ties these ideas together is proportion: effort belongs where the money is. Once you know the dominant line, each lever has an obvious place, whether that is a commitment for steady load, cheaper reclaimable capacity for work that can be interrupted, colder tiers for rarely read data, or a CDN for transfer. Each saving carries a condition you must respect. If you remember one thing: read the bill and find the biggest line before optimising anything."
 },
 "1.1": {
  "intro": "A queue is where work waits when it arrives faster than it can be handled. Any component with finite capacity has one in front of it, even if no queue appears in your diagram.\n\nThis explains most overload behaviour. A request waiting in line is slow even when the server is healthy, and people who treat an unbounded queue as free buffering turn overload into unbounded latency, then timeouts and memory exhaustion. The key ideas below cover where queues hide, how fast they grow, what it takes to drain them, and why waiting counts as latency.",
  "takeaway": "The thread is simple accounting of rates. Whatever arrives beyond the service rate accumulates, and it only goes away when there is spare capacity to work it off, which a system at full utilisation never has. Because waiting is part of the latency a request sees, a growing queue is a latency problem even on a healthy server. If you remember one thing: a queue does not remove overload, so work out how fast it grows and how long it takes to drain."
 },
 "1.2": {
  "intro": "A server's capacity can be described with two numbers: how many requests it can work on at once, called slots, and how long each request takes, called service time. Throughput is not a setting of its own. It follows from those two.\n\nThis model lets you estimate per-instance capacity and explain why servers with the same throughput behave differently under bursts. Quoting requests per second as if it were configurable misses that it moves whenever concurrency or service time changes. The key ideas below cover the throughput formula, what counts as a slot, the few-fast versus many-slow contrast, and what happens when all slots are busy.",
  "takeaway": "What ties these ideas together is that throughput is derived, not chosen. Slots and service time produce it, and the same figure can come from very different shapes, each with its own trade between latency and room for bursts. Once slots fill, requests wait, and that wait sits on top of a service time you cannot go below. If you remember one thing: reason about capacity as concurrency and service time, and change one of those to change throughput."
 },
 "1.3": {
  "intro": "Utilisation is the share of a system's capacity that is in use: the arrival rate divided by what it can handle. Headroom is what remains. The topic is how full you should let a system run.\n\nThe answer is less full than intuition suggests, because latency does not rise in a straight line as utilisation grows. Running near the limit to save money leaves nothing to absorb a spike, a retry burst or a lost node. The key ideas below cover the definitions, how latency climbs with utilisation, why queues form before full load, what headroom is used for, and where the real ceiling comes from.",
  "takeaway": "The thread is that spare capacity is doing work even when it looks idle. It keeps latency away from the steep part of the curve, absorbs variation in arrivals and service times, drains backlogs, and carries the load of a failed node. That is why the ceiling should come from your failure model and from load testing, not from tradition. If you remember one thing: headroom is what lets a system survive a spike or a lost node, so size for it deliberately."
 },
 "1.4": {
  "intro": "Little's Law is a formula relating three averages in any system where things arrive, spend some time, and leave: the number of items inside equals the arrival rate multiplied by the time each one spends there. Given two of the three, you can work out the third.\n\nIt is useful because it holds for any stable system, whatever the arrival pattern or service distribution. Its most common use is sizing pools, where people wrongly work from QPS alone and end up too small. The key ideas below cover the formula, when it holds, using it to size concurrency, and using it backwards to explain exhausted thread pools.",
  "takeaway": "What connects these ideas is that concurrency is a product of rate and time, never of rate alone. That one relation tells you how many slots or connections a workload occupies on average, and it also explains a failure: when a dependency slows down at the same arrival rate, the number of requests in flight rises in proportion until a pool runs out. If you remember one thing: size pools from arrival rate multiplied by how long each request is held."
 },
 "1.5": {
  "intro": "A load balancer sits in front of a group of servers and decides which one receives each request. The rule it uses is its routing strategy, and different strategies react differently when the servers are not all equally healthy.\n\nThe choice matters most when one server is degraded but not dead. It still answers health checks, so nothing removes it, and a strategy blind to how busy servers are keeps sending it a full share. The key ideas below cover round robin, least connections and its trap, consistent hashing, the slow-server problem, and what health checks and the L4 and L7 layers do.",
  "takeaway": "The thread is how much the balancer knows about the state of each server. A strategy blind to load treats a struggling server like a healthy one, while one that tracks in-flight requests steers away from it, yet can be fooled by a server that fails fast and so needs ejection on error rate. Health checks only handle the dead case. If you remember one thing: a slow server passes health checks, so your routing has to notice it."
 },
 "1.6": {
  "intro": "A retry storm is what happens when retries meant to cover occasional failures end up causing more of them. An overloaded service fails some requests, clients send them again, the extra requests add load, and more requests fail.\n\nThis loop is often self-inflicted by adding retries at every layer for reliability. Those retries are harmless while things are healthy and do the most damage exactly when the system is struggling. The key ideas below cover the feedback loop, how layers multiply attempts, backoff and jitter, retry budgets and circuit breakers, and which errors and operations are worth retrying.",
  "takeaway": "What ties these ideas together is that a retry is extra load sent to a system that may already have too much. Each technique limits that load in a different way: retrying at one layer stops multiplication, backoff and jitter spread attempts out, budgets cap their share of traffic, and breakers stop calls to a failing dependency. If you remember one thing: retries amplify overload, so bound them by layer, by timing and by budget before you rely on them."
 },
 "1.7": {
  "intro": "A timeout is the limit on how long a caller will wait for a reply before giving up. Every network call needs one, because without it a slow dependency can hold one of your slots forever.\n\nPicking the value is a trade-off. Too short and you fail requests that would have succeeded and trigger extra retries; too long and waiting calls pile up and the slowness spreads upstream. The key ideas below cover why timeouts are mandatory, setting them from tail latency, the cost of each extreme, shrinking them down the call chain, and deadline propagation.",
  "takeaway": "The thread is that waiting occupies slots, so someone has to decide when it stops being worth it. You choose that point from the dependency's tail latency and the false-timeout rate you accept, and you make sure each hop gives up before its caller does, so nobody works on a request that has already been abandoned. If you remember one thing: timeouts must get shorter going down the call chain, ideally by passing the remaining time budget along."
 },
 "1.8": {
  "intro": "A circuit breaker wraps calls to a dependency and counts failures. When failures cross a threshold, it stops making the calls for a while and fails them immediately, then cautiously tests whether the dependency has recovered.\n\nWithout one, callers keep spending slots on calls that are doomed and keep pressure on a dependency that is struggling. With a breaker but nothing else, users just see errors sooner and nobody notices it has been open for a long time. The key ideas below cover the three states, the two benefits, and pairing a breaker with a fallback.",
  "takeaway": "What links these ideas is protecting both sides of a failing call. The states exist so the caller stops wasting slots, the dependency gets room to recover, and normal traffic resumes only after a trial call succeeds. A breaker is only half the design, though: it needs a fallback so users get something useful, and alerting so an open breaker is noticed. If you remember one thing: failing fast helps only when there is a fallback behind it."
 },
 "1.9": {
  "intro": "Rate limiting caps how many requests a client may make in a given period and rejects or delays the rest. The algorithm you pick decides how the count is kept and how bursts are treated.\n\nThe algorithms differ in accuracy, memory use and burst behaviour, so you need to know what each one gives up. Running a limiter across many nodes also introduces a bug that is easy to miss: checking and incrementing as two separate calls lets concurrent requests both pass. The key ideas below cover token bucket, leaky bucket, fixed window, the two sliding-window variants, and distributed limiting.",
  "takeaway": "The thread is a trade between precision, cost and tolerance for bursts. Each algorithm settles it differently, from letting bursts through up to a cap, to smoothing them out, to counting cheaply with some inaccuracy at window boundaries. Across nodes, the same trade appears as shared atomic state versus approximate per-node limits. If you remember one thing: whichever algorithm you choose, the check and the update must be a single atomic operation."
 },
 "1.10": {
  "intro": "Backpressure is a signal from an overloaded component to whatever feeds it, telling it to slow down. The alternative is accepting everything and silently drowning. Load shedding and admission control are related ways of refusing work on purpose.\n\nThese ideas decide how a system behaves when demand exceeds capacity. Without them, overload hides in an unbounded queue that looks like resilience until latency and memory blow up, and every request suffers. The key ideas below cover what backpressure is, bounded queues, shedding load early and cheaply, admitting work by priority, and why serving the newest requests first often helps.",
  "takeaway": "What ties these ideas together is choosing who is affected by overload instead of letting it affect everyone. A bounded queue forces the choice, shedding makes the refusal cheap and early, admission control directs it at low-priority work, and favouring recent requests avoids serving callers who have probably timed out. If you remember one thing: bound your queues and reject deliberately, because a quick refusal for some is better than slowness for all."
 },
 "2.1": {
  "intro": "The single-database bottleneck is the point at which one database can no longer keep up with the load placed on it. It is a special case because the database holds the one shared truth, so unlike a stateless server it cannot simply be copied.\n\nThe poor response is to jump straight to sharding when a missing index or a cache would have removed most of the load. The key ideas below cover why the database differs from stateless servers, which resources actually run out, the escalation ladder from cheapest to most complex, and how far up it to climb.",
  "takeaway": "The thread is escalation in order of cost. Because the shared truth cannot be copied the way stateless servers can, each way of getting more out of the database buys time at a higher price in complexity, and the sensible path starts with the cheapest fix and stops as soon as the numbers are satisfied. If you remember one thing: climb the ladder one rung at a time, and only as far as the numbers require."
 },
 "2.2": {
  "intro": "Database internals here means how a storage engine holds data and what workload it is built for. The two main structures are the B-tree, which keeps sorted pages and updates them in place, and the LSM tree, which logs writes and merges sorted files later.\n\nKnowing them lets you justify a store by how it behaves instead of by its name. It also explains a common mistake: running heavy analytical scans on the transactional database, where they starve the transactions. The key ideas below cover B-trees, LSM trees, how LSM reads use Bloom filters, and the OLTP and OLAP workloads with their storage layouts.",
  "takeaway": "What connects these ideas is that a storage engine is shaped by the workload it expects. Updating sorted pages in place favours predictable reads, deferring the merge favours write throughput, and row or column layout follows from whether you touch a few rows or scan a few columns across many. Different workloads call for different engines. If you remember one thing: match the engine to the workload, and keep analytical scans off the transactional database."
 },
 "2.3": {
  "intro": "A cache is a small store that sits in front of your database and holds copies of the answers you ask for most often. A read checks the cache first and goes to the database only when the copy is missing.\n\nIt appears in almost every design you will draw, and it fails quietly: copies go stale, the cache fills up, or it empties and the database receives all the traffic it was shielded from. The key ideas below cover the read and write patterns, invalidation and eviction, and the arithmetic linking hit ratio to database load.",
  "takeaway": "Every choice here is a trade between freshness, speed and how much load the database still has to carry. The patterns decide when the copy is written, invalidation decides how stale it may get, and the hit ratio decides what the database sees, which is why a small drop in hits is a large rise in load. If you remember one thing: the database must survive the day the cache is empty, so size it and plan warm-up for that."
 },
 "2.4": {
  "intro": "A read replica is a copy of your database that follows the primary. The primary takes every write and streams its changes to the replicas, and the replicas answer read queries.\n\nIt is usually the first thing you propose when reads outgrow one machine, and interviewers probe it quickly, because the copies run slightly behind. People who miss this show users data that lacks their own latest change, or offer replicas as a cure for write load. The key ideas below cover lag, read-your-writes fixes, synchronous replication, failover and change data capture.",
  "takeaway": "The thread through all of this is that a replica is a copy made at some earlier moment, and each technique is a decision about how much that delay may show. Asynchronous copies are fast and can lag or lose writes on failover; synchronous ones close the gap by making writes wait. If you remember one thing: replicas scale reads only, because every one of them still has to apply every write."
 },
 "2.5": {
  "intro": "Sharding means splitting one dataset across several machines, using a shard key to decide which machine holds each record. Each machine then stores and writes only its own part.\n\nYou reach for it when data or writes no longer fit on one machine, and the interview question is nearly always how you would choose the key. A poor choice sends your most common query to every shard, or piles traffic onto one of them. The key ideas below cover range, hash and directory partitioning, consistent hashing, and the problems of hot keys and cross-shard work.",
  "takeaway": "Each partitioning scheme buys one property by giving up another: range scans against hot spots, even spread against scattered queries, flexibility against an extra hop. None of them removes hot keys, and all of them make work that crosses shards hard. So the key has to follow how the data is actually read. If you remember one thing: list your queries first, then pick the shard key that keeps related data together."
 },
 "2.6": {
  "intro": "The CAP theorem describes the choice a distributed system faces when its machines cannot reach each other. During that network partition it can refuse some requests to stay consistent, or keep answering with data that may be stale.\n\nInterviewers use it to see whether you understand the trade or only the slogan. People who recite the slogan talk as if partitions could be avoided, or stamp one label on a whole database regardless of its configuration. The key ideas below cover what the choice really is, what consistency means here, the PACELC extension, and how quorums make the trade tunable.",
  "takeaway": "What ties these ideas together is that consistency is bought per operation, and paid for in refused requests during a partition and in latency the rest of the time. Quorum settings are the dial, and even a setting where reads and writes overlap stops short of full linearizability. If you remember one thing: partitions will happen, so say what each operation does while one lasts, refuse or answer with possibly stale data."
 },
 "2.7": {
  "intro": "Databases come in different shapes, each built for one kind of data and one kind of question. A relational database handles joins and transactions; others are built for lookups by key, large files, timestamped points, text search, similarity search or relationships.\n\nIn a design interview you are expected to put each kind of data in a store that suits it and to justify every store you add. The mistakes run both ways: forcing images or video into the relational database, or collecting stores that each need syncing and operating. The key ideas below go through each type and what it is for.",
  "takeaway": "The common thread is to match the store to the access pattern, and to count the cost of each addition. Relational is the starting point, and each other type earns its place only when a specific kind of data or query forces it, because every extra store has to be kept in sync and operated. If you remember one thing: start relational and add a specialised store only when a real requirement demands it."
 },
 "2.8": {
  "intro": "Every record needs an identifier that no other record shares. Generating one is easy on a single database and harder when many machines create records at the same moment without talking to each other.\n\nThe question comes up as soon as a design has a table or a public URL, and the choice affects index behaviour, sorting and what outsiders can learn from your IDs. Picking by habit can leave you with a bottleneck, scattered index inserts or guessable identifiers. The key ideas below compare auto-increment, UUID v4, Snowflake-style IDs and ULID or UUID v7, then give the questions that decide between them.",
  "takeaway": "The schemes differ in how they trade coordination, size, ordering and guessability. A shared counter is ordered but central; pure randomness needs no coordination but does not sort; the timestamp-based designs sit between, each with its own conditions. Asking what the ID must do picks the scheme for you. If you remember one thing: an ID that is hard to guess is not access control, so check authorisation on every request."
 },
 "3.1": {
  "intro": "A message queue holds jobs until a worker is ready for them, and hands each job to one worker. Receiving a job does not remove it: the worker has to confirm that the work is finished, and otherwise the job is offered again.\n\nQueues appear whenever a design moves work out of the request path, and the interviewer will ask what happens when a worker crashes or slows down. Without the details you get duplicated work, jobs that fail forever, or a queue that overloads itself. The key ideas below cover at-least-once delivery, visibility timeouts, poison messages, ordering and sizing consumers.",
  "takeaway": "Everything follows from one design decision: the queue keeps a message until someone confirms the work is done. That makes losing work hard and repeating work normal, so the timeout, the attempt cap, the dead-letter queue and deduplication in the consumer all exist to keep repeats harmless and bounded. If you remember one thing: delivery is at least once, so set the visibility timeout above your slowest normal job and make every handler safe to run twice."
 },
 "3.2": {
  "intro": "A stream platform such as Kafka stores events in append-only logs that stay readable after they have been consumed. Each consumer tracks how far it has read, and can go back and read again.\n\nInterviews test whether you know exactly what its guarantees cover. Engineers who assume too much lose per-key ordering when they add partitions, or repeat an outside side effect after a crash while believing they have exactly-once. The key ideas below cover partitions and consumer groups, delivery guarantees, the limits of exactly-once, event sourcing, and windows with watermarks.",
  "takeaway": "The log is the common thread: because events are kept in order and can be reread, progress is just an offset, state can be rebuilt by replay, and recovery means reading again. The price is that anything outside the log can see the same event twice, and that order holds only inside one partition. If you remember one thing: exactly-once covers records and offsets inside Kafka, so writes to outside systems must be idempotent or share a transaction with the offset."
 },
 "3.3": {
  "intro": "Publish/subscribe lets one sender reach many independent receivers. A publisher sends a message to a topic, and every subscription on that topic gets its own copy, with its own backlog and its own acknowledgements.\n\nYou use it when several consumers must each react to the same event, at their own pace. The usual failures are a subscriber that goes down and never receives what it missed, and a handler that cannot cope with a repeated message. The key ideas below cover buffering per consumer, delivery guarantees, ordering by key, filtering at the broker, and push against pull.",
  "takeaway": "The thread is isolation between consumers: each subscription is its own queue, so one slow or offline reader falls behind alone, provided it really has a buffer of its own. Whether delivery is at least once or at most once, and ordered per key or not at all, depends on the product and settings you pick. If you remember one thing: give every subscriber its own buffer, and write handlers that tolerate the same message twice."
 },
 "3.4": {
  "intro": "When one user posts and many others read, the work of building each reader's feed has to happen somewhere. Push does it at posting time, by copying the post into every follower's stored timeline. Pull does it at reading time, by fetching and merging posts from everyone the reader follows.\n\nThis is the centre of any feed or timeline design, and the same choice comes back elsewhere, in search for example. A design based on averages breaks on the few accounts with huge audiences. The key ideas below cover both models, how to compare their total work, what skew does, and the hybrid that results.",
  "takeaway": "Both models do the same job and differ only in who pays: push charges the author once per follower, pull charges the reader once per followee. Comparing total work gives a first answer, and skew then overturns it at the extremes, which is why a hybrid of the two makes sense. If you remember one thing: push for ordinary accounts when reads dominate, and merge the largest accounts in at read time."
 },
 "3.5": {
  "intro": "CQRS separates the model that changes data from the models that answer queries. Commands go through validation and business rules on the write side; queries read from separate read models that are shaped for lookup and contain no domain logic.\n\nIt comes up when one model cannot serve both the writes and every kind of read, and interviewers check that you know its costs as well as its benefits. The read models are updated after the write, so they lag, and a careless update path can lose changes without any error. The key ideas below cover how far the split goes, projections, lag, and when not to use it.",
  "takeaway": "Once reads and writes live in different models, the pattern comes down to the pipeline that carries changes from one to the other. It has to be reliable, which is the job of the outbox and of projections that skip what they have already applied, and its delay has to be hidden from the user who just made a change. If you remember one thing: never commit and then publish as two separate steps; write an outbox row in the same transaction."
 },
 "3.6": {
  "intro": "A saga carries out one business operation across several services without a shared transaction. Each service commits its own step, and if a later step fails, the earlier ones are undone by running compensating actions.\n\nYou need it once a design gives each service its own database, because two-phase commit across services is something most designs avoid. The details decide whether it works: steps in the wrong order cannot be undone, and a careless undo overwrites other transactions' changes. The key ideas below cover compensation, ordering around the pivot, choreography and orchestration, the lack of isolation, and idempotent steps.",
  "takeaway": "A saga gives up the atomicity and isolation of a single transaction and rebuilds what it can by hand. Compensations stand in for rollback, the pivot stands in for a commit point, semantic locks stand in for isolation, and idempotent steps with an outbox let it survive crashes. If you remember one thing: a compensation is a new business action that reverses the meaning of a step, not a restore of the old values."
 },
 "3.7": {
  "intro": "Batch processing runs a job over a whole dataset at once. The job is split into many tasks that run on many machines, and their partial results are grouped by key and combined.\n\nIt comes up whenever a design has to compute something over all the data it holds, or recompute history after a code change. Workers die part-way, a few keys carry far more records than the rest, and a careless rerun can delete data it was not meant to touch. The key ideas below cover MapReduce, fault tolerance by re-execution, shuffle and skew, Spark, Lambda and Kappa, and safe overwrites.",
  "takeaway": "The idea underneath all of it is that the input never changes and each task is a deterministic function of it. That is what lets the framework rerun a dead worker's tasks, lets Spark recompute a lost partition from its lineage, and lets you rerun a whole job after fixing a bug. Your output has to keep the same property. If you remember one thing: write each run so that it replaces only the partitions it computed, and rerunning it changes nothing else."
 },
 "4.1": {
  "intro": "There are two ways to give a system more capacity. Scaling up, or vertical scaling, replaces a machine with a bigger one. Scaling out, or horizontal scaling, adds more machines and divides the work among them.\n\nMost designs reach the point where you must say which way each tier grows, and why. People who add machines by reflex find that throughput stays flat or falls, or that users lose their sessions. The key ideas below cover statelessness, the limits that coordination sets, failure and headroom, why databases usually scale up first, and comparing against one good machine.",
  "takeaway": "Scaling out only pays when the work can be divided, and the ideas here are different views of that condition. Stateless servers divide freely; anything serialised or kept consistent across nodes sets a ceiling; shared state such as a database resists splitting, so it grows upward first. If you remember one thing: before adding machines, check what the work is serialised on, because more instances behind one lock or one row add contention, not capacity."
 },
 "4.2": {
  "intro": "Autoscaling changes the number of running instances automatically as load changes. You pick a metric and a target for it, and a controller adds or removes instances to hold the metric near that target.\n\nIt is the usual answer to variable load, and interviewers look for whether you know its limits. New capacity arrives minutes after it is needed, the wrong metric gives the controller nothing it can steer, and an autoscaler reacting to a struggling dependency can deepen an outage. The key ideas below cover target tracking, choosing the metric, reaction time, scaling in slowly, scheduled and predictive scaling, and safety bounds.",
  "takeaway": "An autoscaler is a control loop with a delay, and each idea is about living with that delay. The metric must respond to added capacity, short spikes must be met by what is already running, scale-in must be cautious, and predictable load can be scheduled instead of detected. The loop also needs bounds so it cannot amplify a failure. If you remember one thing: autoscaling follows load minutes late, so keep headroom for anything faster than that."
 },
 "4.3": {
  "intro": "A content delivery network, or CDN, is a shared HTTP cache that runs in many locations. A request is answered from a stored copy near the user when one exists, and goes back to your origin server only when it does not.\n\nIt belongs in any design that serves static files or public pages, because it keeps most requests away from the origin. Handled carelessly it serves old content, or shows one user's page to another. The key ideas below cover the cache key, the Cache-Control rules, three ways to change content, hit ratio and origin load, what must not be shared, and edge compute.",
  "takeaway": "A shared cache serves one stored copy to many people, so everything depends on deciding which responses are truly the same for everyone. The cache key and Cache-Control express that decision, the hit ratio measures how well it works, and versioned names sidestep invalidation. If you remember one thing: a response that varies by user must be marked private or keyed by user, or the first visitor's version goes to everyone after them."
 },
 "4.4": {
  "intro": "Running in more than one region means keeping copies of your system and its data in separate places, so that losing one region does not stop the service.\n\nInterviewers raise it when they ask about disaster recovery. The trade-offs in cost, latency and data loss are large, and a standby that has never been exercised tends to fail on the day it is needed. The key ideas below cover the recovery strategies, asynchronous and synchronous replication, where each write goes, the three-region requirement for consensus databases, how traffic moves, and the spare capacity that failover needs.",
  "takeaway": "Every decision here puts a price on the same things: how long recovery takes, how much data a failure may lose, and what each write costs in latency and money. Cheaper strategies recover slowly, asynchronous replication loses its lag, and stronger guarantees make writes wait on another region. If you remember one thing: failover only works with capacity and configuration that already exist, so exercise and test the standby regularly."
 },
 "4.5": {
  "intro": "A microservice is a part of a system that is deployed on its own and owns one business capability. A microservice architecture builds an application from many of these, where a monolith keeps everything in one deployable unit.\n\nInterviewers ask when you would split a system, and a good answer weighs both sides. The split buys independent releases and team ownership, and it costs everything that comes with a network between components. Split too early or along the wrong lines, and the services must still be released together. The key ideas below cover benefits, costs, data boundaries, the distributed monolith, service meshes, and starting with a monolith.",
  "takeaway": "The thread is independence: a service is worth its cost only if it can be released alone, and that requires it to own its data. Shared tables or coordinated releases remove the independence and leave the remote calls, the eventual consistency and the operational load. A mesh moves network behaviour out of the code but adds its own overhead. If you remember one thing: start with a monolith and split along a boundary where a service can own its data."
 },
 "5.1": {
  "intro": "A model server takes prediction requests and runs them through a model, typically on a GPU. Compared with an ordinary web server it has few slots for concurrent work, and each piece of work takes tens to hundreds of milliseconds.\n\nAny ML design with online predictions includes one, and you will be asked to size it. Treating it like an ordinary web service leads to endpoints that scale on the wrong signal, or that leave requests waiting for minutes after scaling from zero. The key ideas below cover the throughput formula, dynamic batching and its two knobs, where the gain flattens, and cold start.",
  "takeaway": "GPU capacity is scarce and slow to add, so you gain throughput by filling each batch and protect latency by limiting how long a request waits for one. Batching trades one for the other until the GPU is saturated, and scaling cannot rescue you quickly because a new replica takes time to load. If you remember one thing: choose the largest batch whose measured latency fits the SLO, and keep a warm minimum of replicas."
 },
 "5.2": {
  "intro": "A feature store manages the input values a model uses, called features. It keeps them in two places: an offline store with the full history, for building training sets, and an online store with only the latest value per entity, for fast lookups at request time.\n\nIt matters in ML design answers because training data and serving data must agree. When they do not, a model scores well offline and disappoints in production. The key ideas below cover materialisation, point-in-time correctness, training-serving skew and per-feature freshness.",
  "takeaway": "Everything here serves one aim: the model should see at serving time the same kind of values it saw in training. One definition feeding both stores removes the second code path, point-in-time joins stop training rows from seeing later values, and freshness rules keep online values from being served stale. If you remember one thing: a training row for an event may only use feature values as they stood at that moment, never the latest ones."
 },
 "5.3": {
  "intro": "Inference is running a trained model to get a prediction. You can do it ahead of time for a known set of inputs and store the results, do it when a request arrives, or queue the request and collect the result later.\n\nThis choice shapes the whole serving design, so make it deliberately. Choosing per-request inference because it sounds more advanced costs more and imposes a latency budget that can limit the model; choosing batch leaves nothing to look up for an input never seen before. The key ideas below cover batch, online and asynchronous inference, and the hybrid that most real systems use.",
  "takeaway": "The three modes sit on one line that trades freshness and coverage of new inputs against cost, speed and simplicity. Where you land depends on whether the prediction needs something that exists only at request time, and most systems split the work so that only the last context-dependent step runs online. If you remember one thing: if the inputs can be listed in advance and a day-old answer is acceptable, precompute and serve from a lookup."
 },
 "5.4": {
  "intro": "A model registry stores trained models, each as a numbered version together with a record of the run, data and metrics that produced it. Deployments point at a named alias, and deploying means moving that alias to a new version.\n\nWhen an interviewer asks how you would release a new model safely, this is the vocabulary for the answer. A model can return nonsense with a successful status, so an ordinary health check will not catch a bad version. The key ideas below cover shadow, canary and blue/green rollouts, and how rollback is prepared in advance.",
  "takeaway": "Each rollout stage exposes the new version to more reality while limiting what a failure can cost: shadow puts no user at risk, a canary risks a small share of traffic, and blue/green keeps the old fleet one router switch away. The registry makes each step a move of a pointer between known versions. If you remember one thing: decide the rollback before the rollout, with alarms defined and the previous version still deployed."
 },
 "5.5": {
  "intro": "Data-parallel training spreads one training job across many GPUs. Every GPU holds a full copy of the model and processes a different slice of the data, and the copies combine their results after each step so that they stay identical.\n\nIn an interview about training infrastructure, the weak points are rarely the model itself. GPUs sit waiting for data, added workers bring less speed than expected, and a failure or a spot interruption loses whatever was not saved. The key ideas below cover gradient averaging, scaling efficiency, learning-rate scaling, the input pipeline and checkpoints.",
  "takeaway": "A training job is limited by its slowest stage and protected only by its last save. Communication at the end of every step keeps speedup below linear, an input pipeline that cannot keep up leaves GPUs waiting, and a larger effective batch needs its learning rate adjusted. Failures are expected, so progress has to be saved on a schedule. If you remember one thing: checkpoint model and optimizer state regularly, because the interval is the most work you can lose."
 },
 "5.6": {
  "intro": "An embedding service converts items and queries into vectors, arranged so that similar things end up near each other. Finding similar items then becomes a search for the nearest vectors.\n\nIt is the part of a design that answers requests of the form: find things like this one. Comparing a query with every stored vector grows linearly with the collection, so real systems use approximate search, which gives up some accuracy for speed in ways you have to measure. The key ideas below cover exact and approximate search, recall, the HNSW and IVF index types, quantization, sizing, and re-indexing.",
  "takeaway": "Every index choice here trades the same quantities against each other: recall, memory, build time and query time. Approximate indexes are fast because they skip most of the collection, which is also why they can miss a true neighbour or return too few results once a filter is applied. If you remember one thing: an approximate index does not guarantee recall, so measure it and tune the index settings against a target."
 },
 "5.7": {
  "intro": "A recommendation system chooses a short list of items to show a user out of a catalogue of millions. It works in stages: a cheap first pass picks a few hundred candidates, a heavier model ranks them, and a final pass adjusts the list.\n\nIn an ML design interview the staged structure is the answer the interviewer is listening for. Proposing a single large model that scores every item fails on latency alone. The key ideas below cover the funnel and why it exists, embedding retrieval for candidates, the feedback loop created by the system's own output, and cold start.",
  "takeaway": "The funnel is a way of spending a fixed latency budget: cheap computation on many items, expensive computation on few. Each stage narrows the set so the next can afford more features, and the system then has to correct for learning only from what it chose to show. If you remember one thing: retrieve first, then rank, because a model good enough to rank well cannot score the whole catalogue in time."
 },
 "5.8": {
  "intro": "Retrieval-augmented generation, or RAG, connects a text generator to a searchable store of documents. For each question the system finds the relevant passages and places them in the prompt, so the answer can draw on that text.\n\nIts appeal is that knowledge changes by updating the index, with no retraining. Its weakness is that the answer can be no better than what was retrieved: the right passage may never be found, or may sit among too many others. The key ideas below cover the offline and online steps, why retrieval caps quality, the cost of extra context, and where the latency goes.",
  "takeaway": "The generator can only work with what retrieval hands it, so answer quality is bounded by the search, while cost and speed are driven by how many tokens go in and come out. That is the reasoning behind retrieving widely, reranking, and passing on only a few passages. If you remember one thing: measure retrieval separately from answer quality, because most wrong answers come from a passage that was never retrieved."
 },
 "5.9": {
  "intro": "An orchestrator runs a pipeline of tasks in the right order. The pipeline is a graph in which each task waits for the ones it depends on, and the orchestrator handles ordering, retries and timeouts and records the state of every run.\n\nThe interview point is less the tool than how you write the tasks. The orchestrator will run a task again, and a task that is not safe to repeat duplicates data without reporting any failure. The key ideas below cover re-runnable tasks, data intervals and backfills, step caching, and the validation gates that ML pipelines add.",
  "takeaway": "Since the orchestrator does not care what happens inside a task, safety has to be built into the tasks themselves. A task tied to an explicit data interval that overwrites its own output can be retried, backfilled and cached without surprises, and gates stop a scheduled pipeline from shipping a bad model. If you remember one thing: a task must produce the same outcome whenever it is re-run for the same interval, so overwrite or upsert and never append blindly."
 },
 "5.10": {
  "intro": "An A/B test compares two versions of a system by showing each to a randomly chosen group of users and measuring the difference. Because only chance decides who sees which version, a difference in the metric can be attributed to the change.\n\nFor ML work a live experiment is the only proof that a model is better, since gains measured offline often fail to appear online. An experiment that is set up or read carelessly cannot be trusted. The key ideas below cover assignment, the checks to run before reading results, fixing the sample size, guardrail metrics, and techniques such as layers and interleaving.",
  "takeaway": "A trustworthy experiment is one where chance is the only difference between the groups and where the rules for reading it were set before it began. Random assignment, the validity checks, a fixed sample size and guardrails all protect that, and each shortcut around them makes a false result more likely. If you remember one thing: only a live experiment proves a model, so do not ship on an offline metric."
 },
 "5.11": {
  "intro": "A data pipeline carries events from the systems that produce them to the consumers that turn them into features and training data. Along the way it has to keep the events durably, check them, and cope with duplicates and late arrivals.\n\nIn ML design answers this is where data quality is won or lost. Models tolerate noise, so a broken feature barely moves the overall metric, and the bad predictions become part of the next day's training data. The key ideas below cover ingestion through a log, duplicates, schema and distribution checks, event time with watermarks, and a single code path for features.",
  "takeaway": "The pipeline has to assume that its input will be duplicated, late, out of order and sometimes wrong, and each idea here is a defence against one of those. A replayable log and idempotent writes handle repeats, event-time windows handle lateness, and validation stops bad values before they reach training. If you remember one thing: check the data before it trains anything, because model metrics will not catch a broken feature for you."
 },
 "5.12": {
  "intro": "Monitoring an ML system means watching whether the model's predictions are still good, as well as whether the service is up. A model can keep answering quickly and without errors while its answers get worse.\n\nInterviewers ask about this because ordinary service metrics do not cover it. Latency, error rate and CPU can all look healthy while the model has stopped being useful, and the labels that would show the problem can arrive weeks late. The key ideas below cover silent failure, input skew and drift, proxies for late labels, slicing metrics, and staleness with feedback loops.",
  "takeaway": "Because a degraded model raises no error, you have to look for indirect evidence: inputs that no longer resemble the training data, prediction distributions that shift, and quality on labels as they arrive. Overall figures hide local damage, so each signal is read per slice, and decay over time is to be expected. If you remember one thing: service metrics can all be green while the model is failing, so monitor the data and the predictions themselves."
 },
 "5.13": {
  "intro": "A GPU cluster is a shared pool of machines with GPUs, plus a scheduler that decides which job gets which GPUs. A GPU is handed out whole, and many jobs need several at the same time, which makes the scheduling awkward.\n\nWhen an interview turns to sharing GPUs between serving and training, the question is how to keep them working. Simple scheduling leaves GPUs held but mostly idle, or free but scattered so that a multi-GPU job cannot start. The key ideas below cover whole-GPU allocation, the ways to share one, gang scheduling, fragmentation, and priorities, quotas and spot capacity.",
  "takeaway": "The difficulty comes from GPUs being indivisible by default and from distributed jobs needing all their workers at once, so capacity gets stranded in pieces too small or too scattered to use. Sharing modes, gang scheduling, priorities and quotas are all ways of fitting work onto that capacity. If you remember one thing: allocated does not mean busy, so judge the cluster by how hard the GPUs are working, not by who holds them."
 },
 "5.14": {
  "intro": "Serving a large language model means running text generation for many users on shared GPUs. The model processes the whole prompt in one parallel pass and then produces its answer one token at a time.\n\nInterviewers expect you to reason about it in tokens. Requests differ enormously in size, output length dominates latency, and the memory for each request's attention state, more than compute, caps how many requests share a GPU. The key ideas below cover the latency formula, the KV cache, continuous batching, prefix caching, and further levers such as speculative decoding and cascades.",
  "takeaway": "Everything follows from generation being sequential: each output token is a separate step, and each active request holds a growing cache in GPU memory. The techniques either fit more requests into each step, reuse work on a shared prefix, or cut how much has to be generated. If you remember one thing: plan capacity, cost and rate limits in tokens, not requests, and remember that output tokens are what take the time."
 },
 "6.1": {
  "intro": "This case study walks through how Discord delivers chat in real time. Every event in a server, such as a message sent or someone joining voice, has to reach everyone online in that server within moments and in order, and chat history is kept forever.\n\nIt is useful practice for a chat design question because it shows where the obvious design stops working. One process sending every event to every member falls behind as a server grows, and one busy channel can slow the storage nodes it lives on. The key ideas below cover requirements, the gateway and data model, the process design, fan-out, storage, and failure modes.",
  "takeaway": "Discord's design puts a single ordering point in front of each server and then works to make everything behind it cheaper: full events only to active sessions, fan-out split across relays, identical reads merged, callers that fail fast. Each fix answers a specific bottleneck. If you remember one thing: fan-out work grows with the square of the online count, so a large server cannot be run like a small chat room with more members."
 },
 "6.2": {
  "intro": "Ride dispatch is the problem of pairing a rider who has just asked for a car with a nearby driver within seconds. It combines two very different kinds of data: a constant stream of driver locations, and a trip record that must never be in two states at once.\n\nThe case forces you to separate cheap, short-lived data from data that carries money. Writing every GPS ping to the trip database, or searching all drivers by distance, does not hold up. The key ideas below cover location indexing by cell, pushed updates, batched matching and trip consistency.",
  "takeaway": "Every decision here follows from asking how long a piece of data stays true. A driver position is replaced within seconds, so it can be kept latest-only, indexed by cell, and occasionally lost at little cost. A trip and its driver assignment carry money, so they get transactions. If you remember one thing: keep locations as soft state indexed by cell, and save transactions for the trip and the driver assignment."
 },
 "6.3": {
  "intro": "Video streaming at this scale splits into two jobs. One is deciding: browsing, recommendations and choosing where a client should fetch from. The other is delivering the bytes of the video itself, which has to start within seconds and not stall.\n\nIt is worth knowing because the cost and hard numbers sit in delivery, not in request rate, and candidates who draw a generic pull-through CDN with the cloud in the byte path miss that. The key ideas below cover the control plane, appliances placed inside ISP networks, overnight pre-positioning, content-tuned encoding, adaptive bitrate and how failures are handled.",
  "takeaway": "The design works because Netflix knows in advance what it will serve. That knowledge lets it fill appliances near the viewer off-peak, encode each title at many rates, and leave the cloud to make decisions while the player adapts the rate to the connection. If you remember one thing: the cloud decides and the appliances next to the viewer deliver, so size the system by evening egress and the fill window, not by request rate."
 },
 "6.4": {
  "intro": "A music service has to let you browse and search a catalogue, start a track instantly, keep your playlists and personalise what it suggests. Those needs split into three paths: playing audio files, serving product APIs for the catalogue and playlists, and collecting events that feed personalisation.\n\nThe case mixes plain file delivery with an ML system, and the common mistake is to make playback depend on recommendation. The key ideas below cover immutable track files served from CDNs, the per-type event pipeline, the candidate-then-rank funnel, search beyond term matching and the failures seen in both data and ML.",
  "takeaway": "What holds the design together is separation by rate of change. Tracks never change, so they are files on CDNs; catalogue and playlists are small and read-heavy; listening events flow separately into pipelines that feed batch and real-time models. A slow weekly build does not hold up playback. If you remember one thing: playback is file delivery and must not depend on personalisation, which is a funnel of candidates and a ranker fed by events."
 },
 "6.5": {
  "intro": "A home timeline merges, in time order, the posts of everyone you follow. The design question is when to do that merging: at the moment someone posts, by copying the post's ID into each follower's precomputed list, or at the moment someone reads.\n\nReads vastly outnumber writes, which pushes you towards precomputing, and then the largest accounts strain the precomputed design. Candidates who size the system on the average account miss where the cost actually sits. The key ideas below cover the data model, asynchronous fan-out, the handling of giant accounts, search and ranking, and recovery after failure.",
  "takeaway": "The thread is that you pay for the merge either on write or on read, and the right side depends on the account. For most accounts, fanning out on write makes reads cheap, which suits a read-heavy system; for the few with millions of followers, that write becomes too slow and is better merged at read time. If you remember one thing: fan-out cost is set by the tail of the follower distribution, not the average."
 },
 "6.6": {
  "intro": "A payments system has to charge a customer exactly once, report every outcome and account for every unit of money. The difficulty is not volume. It is that a request can fail in a way that leaves you not knowing whether the charge happened.\n\nThis case tests whether you can design for correctness under uncertainty. The typical error is to treat a timeout as a failure and try again as a fresh request, which can charge the customer twice. The key ideas below cover the payment state machine, idempotency keys, unknown outcomes, the double-entry ledger and webhooks.",
  "takeaway": "Each mechanism here answers the same problem: requests fail midway and events arrive late, twice or out of order, yet money must move once and be accounted for. Idempotency keys make retries safe, the object and its webhooks tell you what actually happened, and the ledger exposes anything missing. If you remember one thing: a timeout or 500 means the outcome is unknown, so reconcile from the object and its webhooks and never retry under a new key."
 },
 "6.7": {
  "intro": "Store-and-forward messaging means the server is a temporary holding point: it keeps a message only until a recipient device has it, then deletes its copy. Each device stays connected to a chat server over one long-lived connection, and the content is encrypted so that the server cannot read it.\n\nIt reverses the usual instinct to make the server the system of record, and a central message archive breaks end-to-end encryption and multiplies storage. The key ideas below cover connections, delivery receipts, setting up encryption with someone who is offline, multiple devices and groups, and behaviour under load.",
  "takeaway": "The common thread is that the server is a relay, not an archive. It holds connections, queues encrypted messages for offline devices, hands out prekeys so a sender can encrypt before the recipient is online, and fans out ciphertext it cannot read. History lives on the devices. If you remember one thing: the server holds a message only until a device acknowledges it, and the sender encrypts one copy per device."
 },
 "7.1": {
  "intro": "A real-time recommendation service returns a ranked feed for each request and shapes the next feed by what the user has just done. It has two halves: a request path that retrieves candidates, ranks them and re-ranks within a latency budget, and a feedback path that turns events into fresh features and model updates.\n\nThe word real-time is where people go wrong, typically by proposing to retrain the whole model every few minutes. The key ideas below cover the scale and API, a per-stage latency budget, how freshness is achieved, fallbacks when something fails, cold start and evaluation.",
  "takeaway": "Everything hangs on one loop: what you serve is tagged with a request ID, joined with the actions that follow, and fed back as features within seconds and as incremental model updates within minutes. The request path stays fast by running retrieval in parallel and ranking only a few hundred candidates, and stays up through a precomputed fallback. If you remember one thing: freshness comes from streamed features and incremental updates, not from retraining from scratch."
 },
 "7.2": {
  "intro": "An inference platform is a shared service that runs many teams' models on a common pool of GPUs. A caller names a model, and the platform decides where that model is loaded, which replica answers, and how much capacity each team may use.\n\nThe obvious design, a pool sized by model count with random routing, performs badly: requests end up paying a full model load instead of a few milliseconds of inference. The key ideas below cover traffic shape, the control and data plane APIs, treating GPU memory as a cache, model-aware routing, tenant limits and failure trade-offs.",
  "takeaway": "The unifying idea is that GPU memory is a cache and the platform's job is to keep requests landing where their model is already loaded. Sizing follows the hot set, very hot models get their own replicas, and quotas and request limits keep one tenant's burst away from the rest. If you remember one thing: size the shared pool for the working set and route each request to a replica that already holds its model."
 },
 "7.3": {
  "intro": "A search ranking system takes a query and returns a short ordered list from a large corpus. It does this in stages: each index shard retrieves a few thousand candidates, a learned ranker rescores the best few hundred, and an expensive model reranks the last few dozen.\n\nEvery stage limits the next: a document dropped early can never be shown, and a slow shard delays the whole query. Training has its own trap, since clicks reflect position as well as relevance. The key ideas below cover NDCG, the sharded index, hybrid retrieval, the ranking cascade, tail latency and biased clicks.",
  "takeaway": "The thread is spending effort where it pays: cheap methods over the whole corpus, costlier models only on the shrinking list that survives, and tail-latency tactics so one slow leaf does not hold everything up. The same care applies to training data, because the system's own ordering shapes the clicks it learns from. If you remember one thing: raw clicks are biased by position, so correct for position before using them as relevance labels."
 },
 "7.4": {
  "intro": "A fraud detection pipeline decides, inside the payment authorisation path, whether to allow, block, review or ask for extra authentication. It combines a model score with explicit rules, and it works under a hard latency budget on a problem where fraudulent payments are rare.\n\nThe difficult parts sit around the model. Labels arrive long after the payment, thresholds are decisions expressed in money, and an outage needs a fallback. Training on recent data labelled by disputes so far teaches the model that fresh fraud is legitimate. The key ideas below cover features, late labels, thresholds and failure handling.",
  "takeaway": "What links these ideas is the gap between when you must decide and when you find out. The score is needed in milliseconds and the truth can take months, so features must be fresh, training windows must be old enough for labels to mature, and thresholds must reflect what each error costs. If you remember one thing: recent payments look cleaner than they are, so train and evaluate only on windows whose disputes have mostly arrived."
 },
 "7.5": {
  "intro": "A RAG system answers a question by first retrieving passages from a body of documents and then having a language model write an answer from those passages, with citations. In a company, each document has its own access rules.\n\nThe interest is in permissions and grounding more than the model. A system that lets restricted text reach the model and asks it to behave will leak, and one that answers with no supporting passage is answering from the model's memory. The key ideas below cover ingestion, the query path, where the permission filter belongs, grounding, and cost and failure modes.",
  "takeaway": "The thread is control over what text reaches the model. Chunks carry their document's access groups, the filter runs inside retrieval, the answer cites what was fetched, and the system refuses when nothing relevant arrives. Cost follows the same shape, since the retrieved chunks are the part of the prompt that changes with every question. If you remember one thing: enforce permissions as a filter inside the retrieval query, so restricted text never reaches the model."
 },
 "7.6": {
  "intro": "A content moderation pipeline decides, for each uploaded image or video, whether to allow it, remove it or send it to a person, and whether to decide before or after publishing. It layers cheap matching against known-bad content, machine classifiers and human review.\n\nThe model is only one part. The thresholds you choose determine how many reviewers you need, and the order of checks determines cost. Designs that rely on classifiers alone, or on hashes alone, each miss something. The key ideas below cover the data path, hash matching, score bands and staffing, queue ordering and the hold-or-publish trade-off.",
  "takeaway": "Each layer handles what the cheaper one before it cannot: hashes catch copies of known content, classifiers handle new content, and people take the cases where the score falls in the middle. Because that middle band is paid for in reviewer hours, setting thresholds is a staffing decision. If you remember one thing: size the human-review band in people, since the share of uploads you send to review decides how many reviewers you need."
 },
 "7.7": {
  "intro": "A feature platform lets many teams define, compute and serve the features their models use. A feature is written once as reviewed code, registered under a name, and then served offline for building training sets and online for fast lookups.\n\nWithout one each team builds its own pipelines and features are rarely reused, and because the serving side is easy to under-size. A single ranking call multiplies candidates by features, so counting requests instead of lookups gets capacity badly wrong. The key ideas below cover the data model, batch and streaming computation, serving fan-out, freshness and multi-team concerns.",
  "takeaway": "The thread is one definition serving two worlds. The same feature code must give training the values that serving would have seen at that moment, and give serving those values fast enough for thousands of lookups per call. Versioning, freshness SLAs and consistency monitoring all protect that agreement. If you remember one thing: size the online path per lookup, not per request, and batch the keys, because one ranking call is candidates times features."
 },
 "7.8": {
  "intro": "LLM serving infrastructure sits between many tenants and pools of GPU replicas that run language models. A gateway checks identity and quotas, a router picks a replica, and each replica generates tokens for several requests at once and streams them back.\n\nIt differs from ordinary request serving : load is measured in tokens, output is the expensive side, and new capacity takes minutes to arrive. Rely on autoscaling for a spike and every tenant's latency degrades together. The key ideas below cover token-based requirements and limits, batching and KV-cache budgets, prefix-aware routing, scaling, overload and failure modes.",
  "takeaway": "The common thread is that the scarce resource is tokens held in GPU memory, not requests. Limits, replica concurrency and scaling signals are all expressed in tokens or cache usage, and because capacity is slow to add, spikes are handled by headroom and by deciding whose work waits. If you remember one thing: autoscaling is too slow to be the overload plan, so keep headroom running and choose in advance which traffic is deferred first."
 },
 "7.9": {
  "intro": "A personalised notification system receives candidate notifications from many teams and decides, for each user, which ones to send, through which channel and when. One decision service sees everything aimed at a user, applies budgets and timing, and hands the survivors to senders for the push providers.\n\nThe obvious objective is wrong. Clicks reward sending more, while the harm from over-sending shows up weeks later as users switching notifications off. A team that optimises click-through alone loses the channel. The key ideas below cover the decision point, per-user state, weighing value against harm, provider delivery rules, reliability and long-lived holdouts.",
  "takeaway": "What ties this together is treating a user's patience as a budget owned in one place. The decision service weighs each send's expected value against its chance of causing a disable, delivery respects provider rules and priorities, and measurement runs long enough to see delayed harm. If you remember one thing: score the chance of a disable alongside the chance of a click, and judge the result with a holdout kept for weeks or months."
 },
 "7.10": {
  "intro": "An autonomous driving data pipeline carries recordings from a fleet of vehicles through selection, storage, labelling, training and evaluation, ending in a validated model. The volume recorded is far too large to upload or label in full, so each stage is mostly about choosing what to keep.\n\nIt is a data problem before it is a modelling one. Treating all driving hours equally spends the budget on uneventful data while the rare events that matter stay buried. The key ideas below cover on-vehicle triggers, storage tiers and scenario search, auto-labelling with human review, versioned datasets, and evaluation before rollout.",
  "takeaway": "The pipeline is a loop of selection: triggers pick segments on the vehicle, search finds the scenarios worth labelling, auto-labellers reduce human work, frozen snapshots make training reproducible, and regression suites gate release. Disengagements on the road come back as new tests. If you remember one thing: do not upload and label uniformly; select on the vehicle and mine with search before paying for labels."
 },
 "8.1": {
  "intro": "In the applied ML design round you are given a loosely worded product problem and asked to design the ML solution. It presumes ordinary serving and pipeline infrastructure and grades your modelling decisions: framing, data and labels, model choice and evaluation.\n\nIt is described as the most common ML round, and people who run the general system design playbook spend their time on APIs and storage that this round rarely rewards. The key ideas below cover the order of steps, suggested time splits, what is graded, what strong answers do and the failures to avoid.",
  "takeaway": "The published frameworks differ in grouping but agree on a sequence that moves from the business objective to labels, a baseline, a model and its evaluation, and they look for reasoning and trade-offs over any one architecture. Following that order keeps your time on what is graded. If you remember one thing: frame the problem and start from a baseline before any complex model, and do not spend the round on database and API detail."
 },
 "8.2": {
  "intro": "Problem framing turns a business goal into something a model can be trained to predict. It means deciding whether ML is needed, choosing a label to stand in for the real goal, and picking the form of the prediction from how the product uses it.\n\nThe real goal is rarely a column in the data, and the proxy you train on is what you get. Optimise the first easily logged signal and the metric rises while the business outcome does not. The key ideas below cover the non-ML benchmark, proxy labels, choosing a formulation, multiple objectives and constraints.",
  "takeaway": "Each idea is a way of keeping the trained objective tied to the real goal: try a heuristic first, pick a proxy knowing where it misleads, match the formulation to how the output is used, and let error costs and launch metrics check the result. The model will optimise whatever you hand it. If you remember one thing: choose a label that rises only when the real goal does, because every proxy misleads somewhere."
 },
 "8.3": {
  "intro": "Metrics are how you decide whether a model is good enough and whether a change should launch. They sit in layers: the business objective at the top, product metrics measured online, and ML metrics computed offline, each tied to the one above it.\n\nA single headline number can hide a useless model, especially when positives are rare, and an online win can come from making the product worse. The key ideas below cover the layers, why accuracy fails on rare classes, precision and recall, ROC against precision-recall curves, calibration, ranking metrics and guardrails.",
  "takeaway": "The thread is that a metric is only useful if it reflects how the output is used and what the business values. That decides whether you need a measure at a threshold, a calibrated probability or a ranking measure at a given cut-off, and it is why guardrails watch for wins that are not wins. If you remember one thing: under heavy class imbalance, report precision and recall at the operating threshold, not accuracy or ROC-AUC alone."
 },
 "8.4": {
  "intro": "Labels are the answers a supervised model learns from, and they come from explicit feedback, observed behaviour, human raters or outcomes that happen later. Most are stand-ins for what you care about, and each source distorts the picture in its own way.\n\nLabel problems look like model problems: results that seem too good, or a model that underpredicts. Treating every click without a purchase so far as a negative is a typical example. The key ideas below cover label sources, delayed outcomes, feedback limited to what the system showed, negative sampling and recalibration, leakage and splitting by time.",
  "takeaway": "The connecting question is what was actually known, and about which examples, at the moment of prediction. Late outcomes, feedback limited to what the system allowed, sampled negatives, leaked information and random splits each break that in a different way, and each has its own correction. If you remember one thing: build every example only from data that existed before its prediction time, and treat a recent example with no outcome yet as unlabelled, not negative."
 },
 "8.5": {
  "intro": "Feature engineering is deciding what information the model sees about each example and in what form. The raw material comes from the user, the item, the context of the request and the history between them, turned into counts, rates, embeddings and scaled numbers.\n\nFeatures built from history can carry most of the signal, and a feature that is not available at prediction time makes offline metrics jump and then fails at launch. The key ideas below cover feature sources, windowed counts and rates, crosses, embeddings and hashing, numeric transforms and the three questions to ask of each feature.",
  "takeaway": "Across all of these, the test is whether the model at serving time gets the same input, in the same form, that it learned from. History supplies much of the signal, encodings make it usable, and the questions about availability, staleness and cost keep it honest. If you remember one thing: for every feature, confirm it exists at request time, because a value known only after the prediction leaks the label."
 },
 "8.6": {
  "intro": "Model selection is choosing which kind of model to train: a heuristic, a linear model, gradient-boosted trees or a deep architecture. The choice depends on your data, your latency and cost budget, and what the product does with the score.\n\nOpening with a transformer, with no baseline, leaves nobody able to tell whether a gain is real or the pipeline is broken, and may cost many times the serving compute of a simpler model that would match it. The key ideas below cover the first model, trees for tabular data, when deep models pay off, expected architectures and selection criteria.",
  "takeaway": "The thread is that complexity has to be earned against something simpler. A heuristic or logistic regression proves the pipeline and sets the bar, trees are the default for tabular features, and deep architectures come in when the inputs call for them and the latency budget allows. If you remember one thing: start with a baseline, and adopt a more complex model only when it beats that baseline on the launch metrics."
 },
 "8.7": {
  "intro": "Training and offline evaluation cover how you split the data, which loss you train with, how you sample and how you check the model before it sees live traffic. The aim is an offline number you can trust.\n\nThis is where quiet errors enter. A random split lets the model train on the future, sampling changes what the outputs mean, and a model can rank well with probabilities far off. Serving raw outputs after downsampling negatives is a standard case. The key ideas below cover time-based splits, ranking losses, sampling corrections, calibration, slice evaluation and the limits of logged data.",
  "takeaway": "Every step here is about making the offline setup match what the model will face and what its output will be used for. Split as you will serve, undo the distortions that sampling introduces, check calibration when the number itself is used, and look below the global average. Even then, logged data cannot show how users will react. If you remember one thing: after downsampling negatives, map predictions back before use, because AUC will not show the error."
 },
 "8.8": {
  "intro": "Online evaluation is testing a model on live users, usually with an A/B test, then continuing to measure and retrain after launch. It answers what offline numbers cannot: how people react to results the old system never showed them.\n\nEarly results mislead. Novelty inflates the first days, a metric can rise because the product got worse, and a model shapes its own future training data. Teams that ship on the first week's lift book gains they do not have. The key ideas below cover launch criteria, reading offline-online gaps, novelty and primacy effects, interleaving, long-term holdouts and feedback loops.",
  "takeaway": "The thread is deciding in advance and then watching over time. Fix the launch criteria before the test, treat a mismatch with offline results as a clue, read the daily difference, and keep a group that never gets the change so you can see what lasts. Reserve some traffic the model does not shape, so it has clean labels. If you remember one thing: do not ship on the first week's lift; watch the daily trend and keep a holdout."
 },
 "9.1": {
  "intro": "Generalization is how well a model performs on data it was not trained on. A model can fail by underfitting, where training error is too high, or by overfitting, where the gap between training and test error is too large. Regularization is the set of techniques that counter overfitting.\n\nIn an interview you should be able to say which side a model is on and name the matching remedy. Details catch people out, such as weight decay behaving differently under Adam. The key ideas below cover capacity, the bias-variance split, L2 and L1 penalties, implicit regularizers and double descent.",
  "takeaway": "All of these ideas describe ways of controlling capacity so that a model fits the pattern and not the particular training set. Penalties, early stopping, dropout and augmentation each restrain the fit in a different way, and double descent shows the classical U-shaped curve is not the whole story. If you remember one thing: compare training and validation error first, because whether both are high or the gap is large tells you which remedy applies."
 },
 "9.2": {
  "intro": "Linear regression predicts a number as a weighted sum of the inputs, and logistic regression passes that sum through a sigmoid to get a probability for a class. Each is trained by minimizing a loss: squared error for the first, log loss for the second.\n\nYou should be able to explain where each loss comes from, not just name it. They also hide a practical trap: on perfectly separable data, unregularized logistic regression never settles. The key ideas below cover the normal equations, the likelihood view of both losses, the log-loss gradient, convexity and the generative-discriminative comparison.",
  "takeaway": "The thread is maximum likelihood. Assume Gaussian noise and you get squared error; assume a Bernoulli label and you get log loss, with a gradient that is simply error times input. For a linear model both are convex problems, so a solver converges from any starting point. If you remember one thing: each loss follows from an assumption about the labels, and logistic regression still needs regularization because on separable data its loss has no minimum."
 },
 "9.3": {
  "intro": "A decision tree splits the data again and again, choosing each split greedily so the resulting groups are as pure as possible. An ensemble combines many trees, by averaging trees trained on resampled data or by adding trees one at a time, each fitting what the previous ones left.\n\nTree ensembles do well on tabular data, so know how bagging and boosting differ and how to tune each. Tuning boosting like a forest leads to overfitting. The key ideas below cover split criteria, bagging, random forests, gradient boosting, XGBoost and LightGBM, and why trees win on tables.",
  "takeaway": "The thread is the bias-variance trade applied to trees. A fully grown tree has low bias and high variance; bagging and feature subsampling average away variance by making trees less correlated, while boosting lowers bias one tree at a time and can overfit if left unchecked. If you remember one thing: more trees are safe in a forest but not in boosting, which needs a small learning rate and early stopping on a validation set."
 },
 "9.4": {
  "intro": "Optimization adjusts a model's parameters to reduce its loss. In deep learning that means minibatch stochastic gradient methods: estimate the gradient from some of the examples, take a step, and repeat, plus momentum, per-parameter step sizes, learning-rate schedules and gradient clipping.\n\nEach part of the usual training recipe has a reason behind it, and you should be able to give it. A common slip is adding an L2 penalty under Adam and calling it weight decay. The key ideas below cover minibatch noise, momentum and Adam, decoupled weight decay, warmup and cosine schedules, large-batch scaling and clipping.",
  "takeaway": "Each technique manages the same tension: a gradient estimate is noisy and only valid in a small region, yet you want to move quickly. Momentum and per-parameter step sizes speed progress, warmup and schedules set how large a step is safe at each point in training, clipping guards against sudden jumps, and batch size trades noise against cost. If you remember one thing: L2 regularisation and weight decay are not the same under Adam, so use AdamW."
 },
 "9.5": {
  "intro": "A neural network is a stack of layers trained by backpropagation: the chain rule applied backwards through the computation graph to get a gradient for every weight. Making deep stacks trainable depends on a handful of choices about activations, initialisation, normalisation and connections between layers.\n\nEach exists to solve a specific training problem, and knowing the problem lets you justify a design choice. Mistakes can be quiet, such as serving a batch-norm model in training mode. The key ideas below cover backpropagation, activations, initialisation, vanishing and exploding gradients, residual blocks, batch and layer norm, and mixed precision.",
  "takeaway": "The thread is keeping signals and gradients at a usable size as they pass through many layers. Activations that do not saturate, initialisation matched to them, normalisation and residual connections all serve that purpose, and mixed precision's loss scaling applies the same care to small gradients in FP16. If you remember one thing: each of these components is there to keep a deep network trainable, so explain a design choice by the training problem it solves."
 },
 "9.6": {
  "intro": "Probability and statistics give ML its language for uncertainty. Bayes' rule converts the chance of evidence given a class into the chance of a class given evidence; likelihood explains where the common losses come from; and p-values and intervals tell you how far to trust a result.\n\nThese ideas come up whenever you compare two models. The usual errors are ignoring the base rate and reading a p-value as the probability that a hypothesis is true. The key ideas below cover Bayes' rule and base rates, maximum likelihood, common distributions, MAP estimation, p-values, confidence intervals, the bootstrap and multiple testing.",
  "takeaway": "The thread is being explicit about what a number is conditional on. A positive test depends on the base rate, a loss on an assumed distribution, a weight penalty on a prior, and a p-value on the null and every other assumption being true. Reading any of them without its condition gives the wrong conclusion. If you remember one thing: a p-value is computed assuming the null is true, so it cannot be the probability of the null."
 },
 "9.7": {
  "intro": "Unsupervised learning finds structure in data that has no labels. The main tasks here are grouping similar points into clusters, finding a few directions that capture most of the variance, spotting points that do not fit, and learning embeddings in which matching items sit together.\n\nThe methods are simple to run and easy to misuse: feeding raw features in mixed units to k-means or PCA lets the widest column decide the answer. The key ideas below cover k-means and its seeding, choosing k, PCA, anomaly detection, contrastive learning and the curse of dimensionality.",
  "takeaway": "With no labels to check against, every method here leans on a built-in assumption about what structure means: squared distance to a centroid for k-means, variance for PCA, ease of isolation for anomalies, matching pairs for contrastive learning. Results are only as good as the fit between that assumption and your data. If you remember one thing: standardise features before k-means or PCA, and do not choose k from inertia, which always falls as k grows."
 },
 "9.8": {
  "intro": "A transformer is a neural network built around attention, in which each position in a sequence scores every position and takes a weighted mix of their values. Around that sit multiple heads, a causal mask for decoders, a tokenizer and a way of encoding position.\n\nYou should be able to derive these pieces and estimate what a model costs to train and run. A frequent slip is quoting the training cost per token as the inference cost. The key ideas below cover scaled dot-product attention, heads and masking, tokenization and positions, parameter and FLOP counts, attention's quadratic cost and scaling laws.",
  "takeaway": "The thread is that a transformer is regular enough to reason about from its formula. Attention's definition explains the scaling factor, the mask and the quadratic cost; the layer structure gives the parameter count; and the parameter count gives the compute for training and inference. If you remember one thing: training costs about 6N FLOPs per token and inference about 2N, where N is the non-embedding parameter count."
 }
};
